    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d211">

                <pb facs="0391" n="319" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg319"/>

                <head>No. 156.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    West</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-04-10"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 10,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to our recent correspondence, touching the condition of affairs in the
                    Indian country near the Canadian frontier, I have the honor to inform you that
                    my colleague, the Secretary of War, has just learned that, in consequence of the
                    movements of the United States military forces, the Canadian half-breeds and
                    Crees have recrossed the line into Canadian territory. Besides escorting a
                    number of lodges of American Indians back to their own reservations, the troops
                    had sent a large body of British Indians across the frontier and burned their
                    lodges.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
